# Overview
#
# Broad reference is the grammar term for a `this`, `that`, or `which`
# that points at a whole earlier statement instead of at one noun (`This
# means the cache stays warm`, `, which means the pool is empty`). The
# reader has to pick which part of the last sentence the pronoun names.
# The fix names the noun (`This retry means`) or joins the two claims.
# The rule sits at Recommended, because the `that` branch also reports
# a `that` that points at one noun.
#
# Expression
#
# `verb` lists the verbs that state a consequence, such as `means`,
# `causes`, and `leads`. `this` counts only before one of those verbs,
# with or without `also` or `then` between them (`this also means`).
# `this is` and `this was` count only before `because`, `why`, or
# `how`. `that` counts only before `means`.
#
# `which` counts only after a comma, and only before `means`, `causes`,
# `leads`, `results`, or the past form of the last three, or before `is
# why`, `is because`, or `is how` (`, which is why the write waits`).
# By default, a Weir match stops at a comma, so `let scope "Sentence"`
# lets the `which` branches cross one.
#
# Four verbs in the list carry a guard. `makes` skips a following `it`,
# `allows` and `lets` skip `you` and `users`, and `gives` skips `you`.
# A space in a pattern is a whitespace token, so each guard sits glued
# to its verb and reads the token right after it.
#
# False Positives
#
# `This is` alone also names a field or a section (`this is set to
# True`), so it stays out unless `because`, `why`, or `how` follows.
# `, which allows` and its kin stay out, because in about half of the
# sampled hits `which` pointed at the noun before the comma (`the
# mirroring feature, which allows`).
#
# The `that` branch also matches a `that` that points at the noun
# before it (`a value that means no value is present`). The rule
# accepts that misfire.
#
# Related Rules
#
# ProseAbility reports `allows you to`, `lets you`, `gives you`, and
# `makes it possible`, so the guards on those four verbs skip the
# phrases. `helps` is left out of the verb list, because the Required
# ProseChatbot reports `hope this helps` and ProseAbility reports
# `helps to` and `helps you`.
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 436 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 20 of 20 sampled
# hits pointed at a whole earlier statement.
expr verb [
    means,
    meant,
    ensures,
    happens,
    prevents,
    causes,
    keeps,
    works,
    matters,
    leads,
    led,
    results,
    resulted,
    (makes!(( )it)),
    (allows!(( )[you, users])),
    (lets!(( )[you, users])),
    (gives!(( )you))
]
expr main [
    (this @verb),
    (this [also, then] @verb),
    (that means),
    ((,) which [causes, caused, leads, led, results, resulted]),
    (this [is, was] [because, why, how]),
    ((,) which means),
    ((,) which [is, was] [why, because, how])
]
let message "Broad reference. Name the noun it points at, or join the two claims."
let description "Flags `this`, `that`, and `which` used as a pronoun for a whole earlier clause."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "This retry means the cache stays warm."
allows "This retry leads to a second write."
allows "This field holds the name."
allows "This change fixes the race."
allows "This lets you pin the version."
allows "Hope this helps."
allows "For a Pod, this is set to True before the sandbox starts."
allows "The flag, which is optional, sets the port."
